The City of Odessa is often referred to as “The Pearl of the Black Sea“.
A visit to Odessa is sure to be unlike a visit to any other city.
Odessa is known throughout the world for its art and culture and has, what is considered to be, the second most beautiful and important opera house in the world.
It is a cosmopolitan city with 150,000 tourists each year from all over the world.
The beaches allow tourists to feel like they are in the Mediterranean.
In the summer they are filled with beautiful girls, music, and visitors sampling tasty food.
Walking in Odessa you can see its history from classical Italian influences to Soviet era apartment complexes.
The port of Odessa has ships from all over the world arriving daily
Public transportation in Odessa city includes 21 tram routes, 15 trolley bus routes, 47 bus routes and 35 minibus routes.
With its beautiful harbor on the Black Sea, Odessa has become Ukraine’s southern window to Europe and an important cultural center.
Odessa is also the home of a Ukrainian naval base and many fishing fleets.
The weather is mild and dry with average temperatures in January of 29 F and 73 F in July.
Odessa looks more like a city located on the Mediterranean, having been heavily influenced by French and Italian architectural styles.
The city is constantly hosting exhibits, symposia, and conferences.
The city has more local character than any other city in Ukraine.
The tourists are attracted by Odessa’s scenic boulevards, the 200 steps of Potyomkin’s stairs, the maritime railway, beautiful sandy beaches along the Black Sea, shops, restaurants, numerous new structures, and the unusual classic architecture of old Odessa.
