Smoked meat is one kind of tender, juicy and super smoky-delicious!
Smoking meat yourself may seem a bit daunting but with some advice on the best equipment and simple tips on how to master the process, you’ll be enjoying delicious smoked meals all year long. A gas, electric or charcoal BBQ smoker is perfect to smoke meat in your backyard.
Smoking cooks, browns, flavour and preserves the meat which is placed it in smoky environment with low, indirect heat for several hours. This long, low cooking breaks down the collagen in the meat, and while that is melting, water gets into the collagen to create gelatin.
Charcoal smokers are the best for a great smoky flavour but can be a bit tricky to regulate the temperature at first.
Which Meat is Best?
Once you’ve had some practice, venture to chicken or turkey, keeping in mind the meat is leaner and can tend to get a bit tough so choose a whole bird and smoke for a shorter time to prevent this. If you want to try seafood, choose a more robust, oily fish such as salmon.
The Smoking Process Smoking meat, like lots of good things in life, takes time and patience so you’ll need to set aside at least four hours but the results are worth it!
*Light your smoker, open the air vents and let it heat up for at least 20 minutes, bringing it up to the ideal smoking temperature which is 105°C – but no hotter than 130°C. Initially, the temperature will be a lot hotter but bring it down by partially closing the vents so the charcoal and wood smoulder.
*Fill the water pan with cold water. This creates vapour in the smoker and allows the meat to cook evenly.
*Smoke meat for approximately 1-1½ hours per 500g of meat. Rotate it every couple of hours, brushing on some more marinade before your turn it.
*Check your meat is properly cooked using a meat thermometer.
See, it’s not complicated at all. Are you ready to wow your family and guests with a tender, delicious smoky meal?
Gas-mate also provides a comprehensive user guide so you’ll be smoking delicious meats in no time!
