People with HIV who are sick need more energy than people with HIV who are not sick.
Increase the frequency of meals and snacks: extra energy may mean eating an extra meal daily or eat more frequently by having small, frequent meals every 3 to 4 hours.
Good nutrition needs supports at all stages – before birth, infancy, childhood, adolescence and adulthood. In this lesson we will be reviewing the nutritional needs of individuals at the various stages of the life cycle, which includes pregnancy (before birth), lactation, infancy, children (young, preschool and school age), adolescents, and adults.
Pregnant women have extra nutritional requirements due to the changes in their bodies and the needs of the growing baby. This is the same for all pregnant women, whether they are HIV-infected or not.
Although more calories needed during pregnancy (extra 200 – 500 calories/day), increasing the nutrition density of the diet is even more important.
FREQUENCY OF MEALS AND SNACKS
